From: Paweł Phan <szop.hektar_1w@icloud.com>
Subject: [PATCH] package/uclibc: claim __cxa_atexit entries without atomics

SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Paweł Phan <szop.hektar_1w@icloud.com>

uClibc-ng's __cxa_finalize() marks an entry of the exit-handler table as
run with a compare-and-exchange, which on MIPS is a load-linked and
store-conditional pair. On a core whose store-conditional never
completes (the MIPS I variant of patch 0001) the pair retries for ever.
The function is in every shared C library: each shared object's start
files call it when the object is unloaded, so a program linked against
the shared C library can reach it at exit.

Add a uClibc-ng patch, package/uclibc/0005-*.patch: without threads,
which is the only configuration the MIPS I variant offers, the entry is
claimed with a plain test and store. Nothing can come between the two in
a program with one thread, so the effect is the same. With threads the
function is unchanged.

The C library's other function with such a pair, _test_and_set() of
<sys/tas.h>, is left as it is: it promises atomicity to processes that
share memory, which a plain test and store does not keep. No program of
the image uses it.

Signed-off-by: Paweł Phan <szop.hektar_1w@icloud.com>
---
 package/uclibc/0005-stdlib-claim-__cxa_atexit-entries-without-atomics.patch | 55 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 55 insertions(+)

diff --git a/package/uclibc/0005-stdlib-claim-__cxa_atexit-entries-without-atomics.patch b/package/uclibc/0005-stdlib-claim-__cxa_atexit-entries-without-atomics.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..f361d32c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/uclibc/0005-stdlib-claim-__cxa_atexit-entries-without-atomics.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: =?UTF-8?q?Pawe=C5=82=20Phan?= <szop.hektar_1w@icloud.com>
+Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 17:51:40 +0000
+Subject: [PATCH] stdlib: claim a __cxa_atexit entry without atomics when there
+ are no threads
+MIME-Version: 1.0
+Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
+Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit
+
+__cxa_finalize() marks an entry as run with a compare-and-exchange, so
+that two threads finalizing at once run it only once. Without threads
+nothing can come between the test and the store, and a plain test and
+store does the same. On a core whose store-conditional never completes,
+the atomic sequence would retry for ever in every program that reaches
+it.
+
+Upstream: not submitted
+Signed-off-by: Paweł Phan <szop.hektar_1w@icloud.com>
+---
+ libc/stdlib/_atexit.c | 15 ++++++++++++++-
+ 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
+
+diff --git a/libc/stdlib/_atexit.c b/libc/stdlib/_atexit.c
+index ced3ee2..96448e1 100644
+--- a/libc/stdlib/_atexit.c
++++ b/libc/stdlib/_atexit.c
+@@ -140,6 +140,19 @@ libc_hidden_def(__cxa_atexit)
+ #endif
+ 
+ #ifdef L___cxa_finalize
++#ifdef __UCLIBC_HAS_THREADS__
++/* Take the entry for this caller only, whatever other threads do. */
++# define claim_entry(efp) \
++	(!atomic_compare_and_exchange_bool_acq(&(efp)->type, ef_free, ef_cxa_atexit))
++#else
++/*
++ * Without threads nothing comes between the test and the store, and some
++ * cores without threads have no working atomic operations.
++ */
++# define claim_entry(efp) \
++	((efp)->type == ef_cxa_atexit ? ((efp)->type = ef_free, 1) : 0)
++#endif
++
+ /*
+  * If D is non-NULL, call all functions registered with `__cxa_atexit'
+  *  with the same dso handle.  Otherwise, if D is NULL, call all of the
+@@ -162,7 +175,7 @@ void __cxa_finalize(void *dso_handle)
+          */
+         if ((dso_handle == NULL || dso_handle == efp->funcs.cxa_atexit.dso_handle)
+             /* We don't want to run this cleanup more than once. */
+-            && !atomic_compare_and_exchange_bool_acq(&efp->type, ef_free, ef_cxa_atexit)
++            && claim_entry(efp)
+            ) {
+             /* glibc passes status (0) too, but that's not in the prototype */
+             (*efp->funcs.cxa_atexit.func)(efp->funcs.cxa_atexit.arg);
